ic papers than i ever you  know had before medical papers in  researching this and you'll find you  know the researchers will say okay that  um the patient said  that they had experienced such and such  such and such many patients said they  experienced such such degree of  resolution in their pain  for whatever whatever the medication  might be to find out if a medication is  effective so they're taking that the  word for it of those patients and  recording it so in that sense anecdotes  are feeding into scientific  investigation even the most formal kind  of scientific investigation at multiple  different levels yes it's absolutely  maddening to me as somebody who  practices science that we would dismiss  anecdote right if i don't have  those anecdotal patterns to work from in  order to figure out what hypothesis  might be worth testing  then there's no way to start and  it occurs to me even just talking to you  right now that we have  medicine functioning at different levels  right we have public health and we have  you know  official guidance on what drugs work on  which conditions and things like this  but the practice of medicine that takes  place in a doctor's office is in fact  supposed to be about the doctor's  ability to work from anecdote and figure  out what it suggests about what you  likely have  what things tend to work for people who  have similar types of diseases doctors  are actually supposed to test hypotheses  at a level that isn't formal  right they're supposed to say well gee  that sounds like you might have a  problem a generalized inflammation  problem if they're a good doctor they  might say let's figure out what's  causing the inflammation a regular  doctor might say let's treat the  inflammation you know  but in any case the point is doctors are  not running studies on  large numbers of patients in general  they are working with  individuals whose presentation is um  individual in it by its very nature  and when they do it right they end 